Benchmark: twist against curl
=============================

bench.py runs twist and curl on the same HTTP downloads and uploads over
HTTP/1.1, HTTP/2 and HTTP/3, against one local nginx, and prints how twist
compares.

  bench/run.sh                      build the image, run everything
  bench/run.sh --only get-big       one scenario (a substring of its name)
  bench/run.sh --protocols h2,h3    some protocols

or, with nginx (1.25 or later, built with the HTTP/3 and WebDAV modules, as
nginx.org's packages are), a curl that does HTTP/3, and a C compiler:

  python3 bench/bench.py --nginx nginx --curl ./curl --twist ../src/twist

Running it
----------

The numbers are for the machine they were made on.  On a laptop, anything else
that runs disturbs them, which is why the workflow (.github/workflows/bench.yml)
runs it on a hosted runner, where nothing else of ours does: by hand, weekly,
and on a pull request that has the "benchmark" label.  The result is in the
job summary and in the artifact (bench.json, every run).

What is measured
----------------

Every scenario is run for each protocol, and for both clients, in turn: curl,
twist, twist, curl, and so on, so that whatever the machine does meanwhile
happens to both.  Each run is a process, and bench/rusage.c, a small program
that starts it, reports its wall time, its CPU time (user and system) and its
peak resident set.  Before anything is timed each client gets the body, or
sends it, once, and the hash is compared: a client does not look fast by
dropping data.

The scenarios (a run is one process; "cold" means a connection of its own):

  get-small         1 KiB, one request, cold: start-up and the handshake
  get-small-x50     1 KiB, 50 requests one after another on one connection
  get-small-par100  1 KiB, 100 requests together (HTTP/2 and HTTP/3)
  get-medium        1 MiB
  get-big           256 MiB (--big-mb)
  put-small, put-medium, put-big    the same, uploaded with PUT

What is reported is the ratio twist/curl, as the median of the ratios of the
pairs, and in brackets the 95% interval of that median (bootstrap).  Below 1
twist is better.  The medians of each client's times follow.  The ratio of
the CPU time is the steadier: wall time on a shared machine wanders.

Read them as follows.  A ratio whose interval lies on one side of 1 is a
difference; one that straddles 1 is not.  Differences of a few percent on a
hosted runner are noise.  --max-ratio N makes the exit status 1 if the whole
interval of a wall or CPU ratio is above N.

Limits
------

- curl is a static build, which starts faster than a program with shared
  libraries, as twist is.  get-small and the first request of the others
  carry that.
- Loopback has no latency or loss: this measures the cost of the programs,
  and not how they do on a long path.
- nginx's certificate is for 127.0.0.1 and both clients verify it.
- HTTP/3 needs both clients to speak it; one that cannot is left out, or
  with --require-protocols fails the run.
